  "metadata": {
    "title": "Infinite modulus between a compact real set and its real complement",
    "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.23441617",
    "publication_date": "2026-10-17",
    "description": "<p>For every compact E contained in [0,1], let F be its complement within the real line and let S2 be the circle of radius 2. We prove that positive planar 2-modulus of all curves joining E to S2 forces infinite2-modulus of all curves joining E to F. This answers the explicit implication in the 2018 version of Hayman-Lingham Problem 7.58, including compact sets of zero length and positive capacity. The proof constructs actual boundary values from a pointwise weighted metric, uses critical trace rigidity to force zero length under finite target energy, and then derives a contradiction by small circular connectors. Both global rectifiability and the stated local-off-endpoints convention are treated.</p><p>Classical foundations and independently checked audit-composed deductions from older general theory are acknowledged. No claim of firstness, a new mechanism, an earlier explicitly printed resolution, or current worldwide openness is made. Priority searches are bounded; full 2019 wording and an 1982 talk body remain unauthenticated. The deposit contains the preprint and a portable TeX-source, written-proof, independent-reconstruction, integrity-verification and priority-audit archive. AI tools were used extensively in research, proof development, literature review, internal adversarial verification and writing. This preprint is unrefereed; no external human peer review, journal acceptance or formal proof-assistant certification is claimed.</p>",
